# Blur the Background in Photoshop

## 1. Duplicate the Background Layer

1. Open the image in Adobe Photoshop.
2. In the Layers panel, select the **Background** layer.
3. Press **Ctrl+J** (Windows) to duplicate the layer.
4. Duplicate the new layer again with **Ctrl+J**.
5. Rename the lower copy **BG**.
6. Rename the upper copy **Subject**.

The **Subject** layer should remain above the **BG** layer.

## 2. Select the Subject Automatically

1. Select the **Subject** layer in the Layers panel.
2. Select the **Object Selection Tool**. It is normally located below the Lasso Tool, but Photoshop may group it with the **Quick Selection Tool** or **Magic Wand Tool**.
3. Open the **Select** menu.
4. Choose **Subject**.

Photoshop will automatically detect and create a selection around the person or main subject.

## 3. Mask the Subject

1. Keep the **Subject** layer selected.
2. In the Layers panel, click the **Add Layer Mask** button.

Photoshop masks the area outside the selection. The **Subject** remains visible, while the rest of the image is hidden.

## 4. Isolate the Background

1. Turn off the **Subject** layer by clicking its eye icon in the Layers panel.
2. Select the **BG** layer.
3. Select the **Object Selection Tool** again.
4. Open the **Select** menu and choose **Subject**.

The subject is selected in the BG layer, which is a temporary selection used to remove it from the background.

## 5. Remove the Selected Subject with Content-Aware Fill

1. Open **Edit > Fill**.
2. Set **Contents** to **Content-Aware**.
3. Check **Color Adaptation**.
4. Set **Mode** to **Normal**.
5. Set **Opacity** to **100%**.
6. Leave **Preserve Transparency** unchecked.
7. Click **OK**.

Photoshop fills the selected subject area with content sampled from the surrounding image.

## 6. Deselect the Subject

Press **Ctrl+D** to deselect the area.

Zoom in and inspect the background. Any outlines or unwanted remnants where the subject was removed can be corrected in the next step.

## 7. Clean the Background

1. Select the **Spot Healing Brush Tool**.
2. Set **Mode** to **Normal**.
3. Set the brush type to **Content-Aware**.
4. Paint over remaining outlines, stains, outlets, or duplicated details.
5. Continue zooming in and working around the subject area until the background looks natural.

Use a small brush for fine edges and a larger brush for broad areas.

## 8. Restore the Subject

Turn the **Subject** layer back on by selecting its eye icon in the Layers panel.

Make sure the **Subject** layer is visible above the **BG** layer.

## 9. Convert the Background to a Smart Object

1. Select the **BG** layer.
2. Right-click the **BG** layer.
3. Choose **Convert to Smart Object**.

Converting the background to a Smart Object allows the blur settings to be changed later.

## 10. Apply the Tilt-Shift Blur

1. Select **Filter**.
2. Open **Blur Gallery**.
3. Choose **Tilt-Shift**.

The Blur Gallery opens with blur controls that can be adjusted in the image and on the right side of the interface.

## 11. Increase the Blur

1. In the Blur Gallery options, increase the **Blur** effect until the background reaches the desired softness.
2. Drag the horizontal focus-control lines to determine where the image stays sharp and where the blur begins.
3. Hold **Alt** and drag the center control point downward or upward, as needed.
4. Position the controls so that the subject remains in focus and the background becomes progressively blurred.
5. Click **OK**.

The blur is applied nondestructively because the **BG** layer is a Smart Object.

## 12. Reopen or Adjust the Blur

You can adjust the blur later:

1. Double-click the **Tilt-Shift** entry in the **BG** Smart Object.
2. The original Blur Gallery settings will reopen.
3. Change the blur radius or control positions.
4. Click **OK**.

## 13. Inspect the Subject Edges

1. Zoom in on the subject.
2. Look for unnatural edges or areas that remain visible outside the subject.
3. Turn off the **BG** layer temporarily if you need to inspect the subject edges against transparency.

## 14. Refine the Subject Layer Mask

1. Select the mask thumbnail on the **Subject** layer, not the image thumbnail.
2. Select the **Brush Tool**.
3. Set **Opacity** to **100%**.
4. Set **Flow** to **100%**.
5. Set **Smoothing** to about **10%**.
6. Set **Angle** to **0 degrees**.
7. Set brush **Hardness** to **0%**.
8. Choose a soft round brush.

Understanding the mask colors:

- **White** reveals or restores the subject.
- **Black** hides areas and leaves more of the background blurred.

## 15. Paint to Perfect the Subject

1. Make sure white is the foreground color.
2. Paint with white over areas of the subject that were unintentionally removed or masked.
3. Switch to black in the foreground.
4. Paint with black over areas that should remain more blurred or were incorrectly included in the subject selection.

To switch foreground and background colors, press **X**.

## 16. Fix Any Missed Subject Areas

1. Temporarily turn off the **BG** blur layer.
2. Zoom in on the subject.
3. Inspect hands, hair, clothing, and any thin or detailed edges.
4. Select the **Subject** mask.
5. Use a small soft brush.
6. Paint with white over missed parts of the subject.
7. Paint with black over any unwanted areas that should remain in the background.

## 17. Complete the Final Refinement

1. Turn the **BG** layer back on.
2. Scroll and zoom across the entire image.
3. Check for straight lines, hair edges, clothing edges, and any remaining outlines.
4. Use black on the foreground to blur or remove an unwanted area.
5. Use white on the foreground to restore a sharp subject area.
6. Continue until the subject and background transition looks natural.

## 18. Save the Finished Image

Save the editable Photoshop project as a `.psd` to preserve all layers and the Smart Filter. Export a copy as `.jpg` or `.png` when you need a finished image for sharing or printing.
